==German==
[[Image:Foto-Exkursion Berlin 2008 05.JPG|thumb|Die deutsche Fahne vor dem Reichstag in Berlin.<br>''The German flag in front of the [[w:Reichstag|Reichstag]] in [[w:Berlin|Berlin]]''.]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|van|van(e)|g=m}}, from {{inh|de|goh|fano}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*fanô||cloth, flag}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*pān-||fabric}}. The shift to the feminine gender began in cognate {{der|de|gml|vāne}} and, through [[Central German]], established itself in the written language by the end of the 17th century.<!--Schiller-Lübben citing it "als masc." with two examples precisely because it's rare, not because it's typical.-->

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈfaːnə/}}
* {{audio|de|De-Fahne.ogg|Audio (Berlin)}}
* {{audio|de|De-at-Fahne.ogg|Audio (Austria)}}
* {{hyphenation|de|Fah|ne}}
* {{homophones|de|Farne|q=some speakers}}

===Noun===
{{de-noun|f|dim=^chen,^lein}}

# [[flag]], [[banner]] {{gloss|any cloth or fabric used as a symbol}}
#: {{syn|de|Flagge|Banner|Standarte}}
# {{lb|de|figurative}} [[idea]], [[ideal]]
#: {{syn|de|Idee|Prinzip|Motto}}
# {{lb|de|colloquial}} the [[notice]]able [[smell]] of [[alcohol]] on one's [[breath]]
#: {{syn|de|Alkoholfahne|Alkoholgeruch}}
# {{lb|de|printing}} [[galley proof]]
#: {{syn|de|Druckfahne}}
# {{lb|de|ornithology}} [[vane]] {{gloss|flattened, web-like part of a feather}}
#: {{syn|de|Federfahne|Vexillum}}

====Usage notes====
* Flags of nations or ships are more commonly called {{m|de|Flagge}}, but {{m|de||Fahne}} (being the more general term) is also possible.

